    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M5 V18: Circular orbits. How does gravity maintain a circular orbit? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Here's the trick. In its first second, any projectile falls about four point nine metres. And Earth's surface curves away below a straight line by about five metres over the first eight kilometres you travel. So a ball launched sideways at about eight kilometres per second falls five metres in that first second, and the ground drops away five metres too. By then its direction has turned a little, so the next second starts along a new straight line, and the same thing happens again. It keeps falling, and it keeps missing. That's an orbit, falling around Earth, not into it. Once it's moving, the only force on it is gravity, pointing to Earth's centre. In a circular orbit, that force is always at right angles to the velocity. As we saw with circular motion, a sideways force like that changes the velocity's direction but not its speed, and it does no work. So gravity is the inward resultant force. Centripetal force isn't an extra force here, it's the job gravity is doing. So a satellite doesn't need its engines running to stay up. That gives us the orbital speed. For a circle of radius r, the inward force needed is m v squared over r. Gravity supplies big G M m over r squared. In a stable circular orbit, they're equal. The satellite's mass m is on both sides, so it cancels. Multiply both sides by r, and v squared equals big G M over r. So v equals the square root of big G M over r, with r measured from Earth's centre.",
    "conversationalApproach": "Imagine a launcher on top of a mountain so tall it pokes right out of the air. Fire a ball sideways, and it curves down and hits the ground a little way off. Fire it faster, and it lands further round the planet. Newton asked this over three hundred years ago: what happens if you keep firing it faster and faster? Have a think. Here's the trick. In its first second, any projectile falls about four point nine metres. And Earth's surface curves away below a straight line by about five metres over the first eight kilometres you travel. So a ball launched sideways at about eight kilometres per second falls five metres in that first second, and the ground drops away five metres too. By then its direction has turned a little, so the next second starts along a new straight line, and the same thing happens again. It keeps falling, and it keeps missing. That's an orbit, falling around Earth, not into it. Once it's moving, the only force on it is gravity, pointing to Earth's centre. In a circular orbit, that force is always at right angles to the velocity. As we saw with circular motion, a sideways force like that changes the velocity's direction but not its speed, and it does no work. So gravity is the inward resultant force. Centripetal force isn't an extra force here, it's the job gravity is doing. So a satellite doesn't need its engines running to stay up.",
